  'html' => '<p><em>Eileen and the Baileys are a fictional family based on <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dijMKwZMU2Q">Chuck Schumer\'s imaginary friends, Joe and Eileen Bailey</a>, who were covered in a special on Last Week Tonight by John Oliver. Who our Home Repair Requestors are is private, protected, and privileged information, and we haven\'t bothered to ask anyone to let us feature their home repair story, yet.</em></p>
<hr />
<h2>Meet Eileen Bailey</h2>
<p>Eileen Bailey is a home-owner in Swannanoa. The Baileys have lived in Buncombe County for twenty-five years. Before Hurricane Helene, the Bailey household was a space of memories and event hosting for family holidays. While the Baileys never had a ton of money, they had everything else.</p>
<p>Then Hurricane Helene happened. Many of the Baileys\' neighbors\' homes were completely devastated beyond repair. The Bailey household had a tree fall on it. No one was hurt. But there was catastrophic damage to the living room. A tree literally fell through it! The rest of the house was undamaged, but the flooding and opening created by the impact made the home unlivable and unprotected from the elements.</p>
<p>Eileen heard about ARCHR\'s Home Repair Program and submitted an application. In the aftermath of the storm, there was no electricity, water, or internet—sometimes for weeks, some places for months. So after the first opportunity the Baileys had to escape and charge their phones, they returned to take pictures of the damage and begin their intake application with ARCHR.</p>
<p>ARCHR has been operating since 2021, and when the storm hit, we already had the intake and management infrastructure to start running. This is why we were successful: because a plan was already in place for when disaster hit.</p>
<p>The Baileys could complete their intake application piecewise, saving their progress along the way. They could work on the application offline and submit it once they could go to their local public library, which had Wi-Fi internet up and running. When they finished and submitted the intake, a partnership of non-profits and aid workers was ready to receive it and mobilize.</p>
<p>Upon submission, a series of events was triggered. Several organizations committed to doing home repairs were alerted. Each had different eligibility criteria for taking on the repair. Because the Baileys were at 80% of the Average Median Income, and because Joe Bailey, a disabled textile worker, resides there, they were eligible for repair assistance with Disability Works, a local agency specializing in families with disabled members. That agency agreed before a storm happened that they would mobilize resources, staff, and volunteers after a disaster, towards home repair assistance. A dozen other organizations signed onto this pact before a disaster hit.</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. The Big Picture: What This Platform Does</h2>
<p>ARCHR connects people who need home repairs with people who can do them. The platform handles case management, document storage, financial tracking, and communication.</p>
<p><strong>The full journey:</strong> application → eligibility → assessment → planning → work → completion</p>
<p><em>For Eileen, this meant she could focus on her family while the system coordinated the repair of her home.</em></p>
